use /lib
use /ui
from /scope/project-data import ProjectData
from /scope/application/Body import Body
from /scope/prompt-mode import PromptEditor
from /scope/prompt-mode/chat-input import ChatInput
from /scope/prompt-mode/message-list import MessageList
from /scope/prompt-mode/modes import PromptModes
from /scope/prompt-mode/queue-list import QueueList
from /scope/prompt-mode/sending import PromptSending
from /ui/components/growing-text-input import GrowingTextInput
from /ui/components/scrollbar import Scrollbar

export ui-component TasksTab:
    pitch:
        The right sidebar's Tasks tab: every task of the project at a glance,
        what has run, what is running, and what is queued, where any of them
        can be looked into, edited, and adjusted, and a chain given more
        steps.

    description:
        The tab is one scrolling column, with a @{Scrollbar}, read top to
        bottom as a timeline, in three groups, each headed by a small muted
        label with its count: "Previous", every task that has run, oldest at
        the top, as the @{PromptEditor}'s previous tasks are, with their
        filters and batch actions along the group's heading; "Running", every
        task under way, in either lane; and "Queued", the queue, in its order,
        as the @{QueueList} says, reordered by dragging, its auto-send
        switch and "Send next" button along its heading. A group with nothing
        in it shows its heading alone, muted, "No queued prompts" and the
        like.

        Every level of the tab remembers where it was scrolled, and coming
        back to it, however, finds it just as it was left. The list keeps
        its scroll when a task is opened from it and the back arrow or
        <Escape> returns to it, when the sidebar slides back and out again,
        when it is closed and opened by hand, and when another project is
        shown and this one shown again, each open project keeping its own.
        Each open task keeps its own scroll, its Prompt expanded or clipped,
        and its Steps section as it was, for as long as the application
        runs, so opening it again, from the list, a step of its chain, View
        all, or anywhere else, shows it where it was left; going from a
        chain to one of its steps and back returns to the chain where it was.
        The list is scrolled on its own only the first time it is shown for
        a project in a launch, so the Running section, or the start of the
        Queued one when nothing runs, is in view, and when a task is opened
        from elsewhere, as from the running activity list, which scrolls it
        into view. Rows added, removed, or moved above what is in view, as
        tasks finish and move from Running to Previous, never shift the rows
        in view: the scroll is kept on the row at the top of the view, not
        on a number of pixels.
        The three groups are accordion sections, each collapsible on its
        own. A section's heading is a row 28 pixels tall, on the theme's
        raised surface, a step apart from the rows: a chevron at its left,
        pointing right while collapsed and down while expanded, then its
        title and count, then, at its right, its own controls, Previous's
        filter and batch actions and Queued's auto-send switch and "Send
        next". Clicking the heading, anywhere but its controls, expands or
        collapses that section alone; any number can be expanded at once,
        and collapsing one takes its rows away and leaves only its heading.
        Previous starts collapsed and Running and Queued expanded, each time
        a project is opened; otherwise each stays as it was left while the
        application runs, each open project keeping its own. A collapsed
        section still counts its tasks, its controls still work, and opening
        one of its tasks from elsewhere, as from the running activity list,
        expands it to show that task.

        All three headings are always in view, however far the tab is
        scrolled, so each section's controls are always in reach. A heading
        whose section starts above the visible rows is pinned at the top of
        the tab, and one whose section starts below them is pinned at its
        bottom, in their order: scrolled down into Running, the Previous
        heading is pinned at the top, the Running heading beneath it, and the
        Queued heading at the bottom; scrolled into Queued, Previous and
        Running are pinned at the top, one beneath the other. A heading never
        pushes another out of view; they stack, each 28 pixels, so the rows
        scroll in the space left between the headings pinned above and
        below. A heading in its place among the rows sits there as any row
        does, and is pinned only once its place leaves the visible rows.
        Clicking a pinned heading, anywhere but its chevron and controls,
        scrolls the list to that section's start; clicking its chevron
        expands or collapses it, as clicking a heading in its place does.

        Each heading carries its section's controls, whichever place it is
        pinned in: Previous's selection, its "N selected", its batch
        actions, "Send N to Spec", "Send N to Code", and "Clear", while any
        task is selected, and its filter dropdown; Running's "Cancel all",
        while more than one task runs; and Queued's auto-send switch and
        "Send next". Where a heading is too narrow for all of them, its
        title gives way first, cut off with an ellipsis, and then its count,
        never its controls, the batch actions folding into a "N selected"
        menu once even that isn't enough.

        A pinned heading draws over the rows with its own surface, never
        letting them show through, and is drawn once, never twice where it
        is pinned and where it lies. Previous's day divider sticks just
        below the Previous heading only while Previous's rows are what is in
        view beneath it.

        The list's @{Scrollbar} is pinned, as every scrollbar is: a column
        along the tab's right edge, always shown, whether or not the list has
        anything to scroll, running the tab's full height, from its top to
        its bottom, beside the pinned headings as well as the rows. It never
        scrolls with the list, never appears only while hovering or
        scrolling, and is never laid over the rows. The headings, pinned or
        in place, the day dividers, and every row end at the column's left
        edge, never running beneath it, so the column takes the same 18
        pixels of width from all of them and their right-hand parts, the
        times and the headings' controls, line up against it. Its thumb is
        sized and placed from the whole list, the rows hidden beneath pinned
        headings and collapsed sections left out, so scrolling it to its
        bottom shows the last row whole. It has no lock to the bottom, since
        the list isn't output that keeps arriving.

    days:
        Within Previous, tasks are grouped by the day they were sent, in the
        user's local time, each day headed by a divider row, 28 pixels tall
        like every row, its label in small muted text set in to line up with
        the rows' text: "Today", "Yesterday", the weekday for the rest of the
        past week, as "Tuesday", and the date beyond that, as "3 October",
        with the year added once it isn't this one, as "28 September 2025",
        then, muted after it, how many tasks that day holds. Days run oldest
        to newest, as the tasks do, and only days with a task are shown; a
        chain is under the day its first step was sent. While filters are on,
        a day with no task shown is left out, and its count is of the tasks
        shown. The labels follow the clock: one reading "Today" reads
        "Yesterday" once midnight has passed, without reopening the tab.

        A day's divider is sticky beneath Previous's own heading: while its
        tasks are scrolled through, it stays pinned just below that heading,
        until the next day's divider pushes it out, so the day of the rows
        in view always shows. Day dividers take the place of the session
        dividers the previous tasks had; a session that starts a new
        conversation shows a small muted mark at its first row's right
        instead, its tooltip saying it started a new conversation.

    rows:
        Every task is one row, 28 pixels tall, as a queued prompt's is, and
        every row in every section is laid out in the same columns, so their
        parts line up down the whole list: a bar in its mode's colour, as the
        @{PromptModes} colours it, 4 pixels wide with corners rounded 2
        pixels, inset from the row's edges, 4 pixels from its left and 6
        from its top and bottom, so it stands as a short rounded pill rather
        than running edge to edge, and the bars of rows one above another
        never touch;
        then a lead column 20 pixels wide, holding a Previous row's checkbox,
        a Queued row's drag grip, and nothing in a Running row, the column
        kept empty rather than closed up where there is nothing in it; then
        a status column 20 pixels wide, a spinner while it is under way, a
        tick, cross, or dash once it has ended, or, queued, its place in the
        queue; then the first line of its prompt, cut off with an ellipsis;
        then, at its right, muted, how long it took, or has been running, as
        the @{MessageList} writes it, right-aligned in a column of its own,
        and any buttons the row has after it. So a row's status and its text
        start at the same place whether it has a checkbox, a grip, or
        neither, and so do the section headings' titles, set in from their
        chevrons to line up with the rows' text.

        A chain, or any task of more than one step, is one row like any
        other, its status the chain's as a whole, with its step count muted
        beside its time. In Previous and Queued its steps are never listed
        in the list; they are shown once it is opened, as its chain steps
        say. While it is Running, it is expanded in the Running group: its
        row, then a row for each of its steps beneath it, in the order they
        run, sent or still to send, each a 28 pixel row in the same columns,
        its text set in 16 pixels further and labelled "Spec", "Code", or
        "Spec follow-up" in that step's mode colour, with the step's own status, a
        tick, cross, or dash for one that has ended, a spinner for
        the one running, "Queued" with its place in its lane's queue for
        one waiting there, and a muted "Next" for one that will be sent once
        the step before it finishes, and "Waiting" for any after that, every
        step listed from the moment the chain is sent, as its chain steps
        say, each with its own time. A step being
        queued shows here, as part of its chain, rather than among the
        queued prompts, though it keeps its place in its lane's queue. The
        steps can't be collapsed, and once the chain is over they go with it
        into Previous as a single row. Clicking the chain's row, or any of
        its steps, opens the chain, its Steps section showing the same.

        A chain's rows are joined by its colour: in place of each row's own
        bar, one bar in the Chain colour, purple, 4 pixels wide with corners
        rounded 2 pixels, runs unbroken down the left of the chain's row and
        every step row beneath it, Spec, Code, Spec follow-up, and any step
        added, from 6 pixels below the top of the chain's row to 6 pixels
        above the bottom of its last step's, across the gaps between the
        rows, so the chain reads as one block. The steps' own modes show in
        their labels' colours, never in a bar of their own. It is drawn the
        same in a chain's Steps section once opened, and grows or shrinks
        with the chain as a step is added. A single task, not part of a
        chain, keeps its own bar.
        Clicking a row opens it, as below, and the row stays highlighted
        while it is open. A row in Previous opens in the tab like any
        other, done, failed, cancelled, or not recorded alike, and its
        output opens in a body tab of its own at the same time, so every
        previous task can be read whole, its header, output table, and
        changed files, however old, as long as its record is in the history.
        One whose record can't be read opens all the same, saying why,
        muted, in place of its output. Hovering a row shows its prompt's first lines in
        its tooltip.

    virtualized:
        The list is virtualized: however many tasks the project has run, it
        lays out and draws only the rows in view, and a few rows either side
        of it, never every row, so opening the tab, scrolling it, resizing
        the sidebar, filtering, and new tasks arriving cost the same with ten
        thousand previous tasks as with ten. Every row is 28 pixels tall,
        section headings included, so where each
        row lies is known from its place in the list alone, without laying
        out the rows before it, and the scrollbar's thumb is sized and placed
        from the whole list's height, as though every row were drawn.

        A row is drawn only from what its row shows, its mode, status, first
        line, and time, read from each task's history record once and kept,
        never by reading a record's output, lines, or snapshots while
        scrolling; those are read only when a task is opened. Running rows
        update their time and spinner in place, redrawing only what is in
        view. Scrolling to a row, as when the tab opens on Running, a filter
        changes, or a queued row is dragged past the edge, jumps straight to
        its place without drawing the rows between. A row scrolled out of
        view and back is the same as it was, its
        selection and highlight kept. A pinned heading sits over the list
        without moving any row, so where each row lies is unchanged by it.

        Scrolling past either end does nothing: with the list at its top or
        its bottom, a wheel, trackpad, or drag that goes further leaves every
        row exactly where it is, frame after frame, with no stretch, bounce,
        or nudge of even a pixel and back, and draws nothing anew, since
        nothing moved. Its scroll position is clamped to its ends before a
        frame is drawn, never drawn past an end and corrected the frame
        after, and kept in whole device pixels, so a row never shimmers
        between two places. What lies at an end stays at it: a running row's
        time ticking, a task finishing, a row added or removed elsewhere in
        the list, or the tab being resized never makes the rows in view jump
        and settle. A list scrolled to its bottom stays at its bottom as rows
        are added, and one at its top stays at its top. The list counts as
        taller than the tab only once it overflows it by a whole pixel, as
        the right sidebar's panels do, so a list that fits never scrolls
        at all.

        Scrolled to its bottom, the last row shows whole, its bottom edge at
        the bottom of the tab's visible area, never cut off by even a pixel,
        and nothing lies beneath it but the list's own bottom padding of 8
        pixels. The list's height is the sum of its rows, headings and day
        dividers included, and that padding, measured in the same device
        pixels the rows are drawn at, and its scrollable height is that less
        the height of the area it is drawn in: the right sidebar's own
        visible area, never an area that counts a sticky heading, the open task's back bar,
        or the scrollbar's own room as part of the list. Headings pinned at the
        bottom take room from the list's visible area, as those at the top
        do, so scrolled to the bottom, the last row still shows whole above
        any heading pinned there, and none ever hides a row. However the tab is resized, by the window,
        the sidebar's panels, or the sidebar's width, the bottom is worked
        out anew in that frame, so a list scrolled to its bottom still shows
        its last row whole.
    details:
        An open task takes the tab's place, with a back arrow and the task's
        mode and name along its top, the arrow and <Escape> going back to the
        list as it was left. Opening a task that has run or is running also
        opens it in a tab of its own in the body, as a file opens, as the
        @{Body} says, showing its header and output there, unless it is
        a task the Chat tab shows, one running or the one it shows while
        none runs, when Chat is selected instead, with that task selected in
        its header. What the open task shows, top to bottom:

        \\
        - Status: its status label and how long it took, or has been running, as the MessageList writes them, on one line
        - Prompt: its prompt, as it was typed, in a GrowingTextInput, and beneath it what is attached to it
        - Steps: for a chain, its steps, as its chain steps say, with Add step; a task that isn't a chain has no Steps section
        - Time: where its time went, as its time breakdown says, once it has started
        - Details: the rest at a glance, its lane, its place in the queue while it waits, the conversation it carries on or that it starts a new one, its model and effort, and once it has run, how many tool calls it made, its errors, its tokens and their cost, and how many files it changed, each a labelled figure, those that don't apply left out
        - Parameters: its mode, its model and effort, its Slice toggle, and whether it starts a new conversation, each as the chat input shows it
        - Actions: Resend, Send to Spec or Send to Code, Pause while it runs or Resume while it is paused, Cancel while it is under way, paused, or queued, and Open output, as the PromptSending gives them
        \\

        A long prompt is clipped: past 8 lines, as they wrap at the tab's
        width, the Prompt section shows its first 8 and fades the last of
        them into the section's background, with a "Read more" link beneath,
        in the theme's accent, with a down chevron. Clicking it shows the
        whole prompt, the link reading "Show less" with an up chevron, which
        clips it again, scrolling the tab back to the Prompt section if it
        had scrolled past it. A prompt of 8 lines or fewer shows whole, with
        no link. It starts clipped each time a task is opened. Clicking into
        a queued task's prompt to edit it shows it whole, as it is being
        typed in, and it is clipped again once the task is opened afresh.
        Clipping only changes what shows: its attachments are listed beneath
        either way, and copying or editing takes the whole prompt.

        A queued task is edited right there: its prompt can be typed in, its
        attachments removed, and its parameters changed, its mode among
        those of the tabs, a change of lane moving it to that lane's queue in
        its place. Each change is saved with the project as it is made, as
        saving a queued prompt's edit is, and while it is being changed the
        task is held, never sent, as a queued prompt being edited is; going
        back, or opening another, lets it go. A task that has run, or is
        running, can't be changed: its prompt and parameters read as they
        were sent, and "Edit and resend" puts them in the open task as a new
        queued prompt, ready to change, sent once "Send" is clicked. The
        chat input's own <Up> editing still works as the @{ChatInput}
        says, and opens the same task here.

    chainSteps:
        An open Chain task lists its steps in its Steps section, beneath its
        prompt and above its details, in the
        order they run, each a row as in the list, with its status: its spec
        step, its code step, and its spec follow-up when it has one. Every
        step it will run is listed from the moment the chain is sent, while
        its spec step is still building or running, before any later step
        has been sent: a step not yet sent is listed all the same, its status
        a muted "Next" for the one that follows the step running and a
        muted "Waiting" for any after it, its prompt the chain's, and its
        time blank until it starts. Each step's row changes in place as the
        step is sent, queued, runs, and ends, and the list never grows or
        reorders as a step is sent, only once a step is added, or a spec
        follow-up is asked for, when it is added at its end. The same holds
        for the chain's expanded rows in the Running group, and for any task
        of more than one step. An "Add
        step" button beneath them adds a step of its own after the last: a
        Code or a Spec step, chosen from a small menu, with a prompt of its
        own, typed in the step's row as it expands, its model and effort
        those of the chain until changed. An added step runs once the step
        before it finishes, in its own mode's lane and place, as every step
        of a chain does, told its own prompt, the chain's prompt as typed,
        and what the step before it replied, as the spec-to-code prompt
        tells a code step, so a chain can be carried on, as a further code
        change once the spec is built, or a spec tidy-up after the code. A
        step not yet sent can be edited, moved among the others not yet
        sent, or removed; one sent reads as it was. Steps are saved with the
        chain, in its hidden anchor, as the @{PromptSending} keeps a
        queued prompt, so a chain keeps its added steps across restarts, and
        a step whose step before it failed or was cancelled is never sent, as
        none of a chain's are.


    timeBreakdown:
        An open task that has started, and an open chain or step, has a Time
        section, beneath its Steps, or its Prompt where it has none, and
        above its Details, showing where its time went, so what is slow can
        be seen at a glance: waiting on the model, building the spec,
        running commands, and the rest.

        Its time under way is split into kinds, each with a colour of its own,
        drawn from the theme's chart colours, distinct from one another in light
        and dark mode and never a mode's colour, so a kind looks the same in
        every task:

        \\
        - Spec build: building the spec ahead of it, and after it for a spec step
        - Preparing: making its sandbox and filtered repository, and the snapshots taken as it started and ended
        - Model: the harness waiting on the model, thinking and writing, between tool calls
        - Commands: shell commands it ran and waited on, a build or tests among them
        - Files: reading, searching, and editing files
        - Subagents: subagents it waited on
        - Web: fetching and searching the web
        - Other: any other tool call, and time that fits none of these
        \\

        Each kind's time is worked out from the times its record keeps, as
        the @{ProjectData} says: each phase from its start to its end, and
        each tool call from when it was made to when its result came,
        counted as its kind; Model is the time between, from each turn's
        start, or a tool call's result, to the next tool call or the turn's
        end. A subagent's own tool calls count as Subagents, not as their own
        kinds, and background tasks that ran beside the main thread are
        never counted twice: only the time the task itself waited on them.
        Time while paused is left out, as is the gap between a chain's steps,
        and time spent waiting in the queue before it was sent: the chart
        starts when the task was sent from the queue, or straight away where
        it never queued, so its kinds' shares are of the time it was under
        way. How long it waited in the queue is given beneath the timeline,
        muted, as "Queued 2:14 before it started", left out where it never
        waited.

        Along the top of the section, a single bar, the section's full width
        and 12 pixels tall, with corners rounded 3 pixels, split into a
        segment per kind in the order above, each as wide as its share of
        the whole, a pixel of the section's surface between segments, kinds
        with no time left out. Beneath it, a legend, one line per kind with
        time, in order of the time it took, largest first: a square of its
        colour, its name, its time, as the @{MessageList} writes times, and
        its share, muted, as "Model 4:12 · 61%". The largest is in the
        theme's text colour and the rest muted, so what took longest stands
        out. Hovering a segment, or its legend line, lightens the other
        segments and shows a tooltip giving the kind's time, its share, and,
        for Commands, Files, Subagents, and Web, how many calls and the
        slowest of them, as "npm run build, 1:38".

        Beneath the legend, a timeline of the task from start to end, the
        section's full width and 24 pixels tall: each phase and tool call as
        a bar at its place along it, in its kind's colour, so long waits and
        when they came can be seen, with a muted time at either end. Hovering
        a bar names what it was and how long it took, and clicking a tool
        call's bar opens the task's output scrolled to that row.

        While the task runs, the section follows it, the kind under way
        growing in place and the timeline extending to now. A chain's Time
        section shows its steps' time together, the bar split by kind across
        all of them, and the timeline marking where each step began; each
        step's own section shows its own. A task from before its phases were
        kept shows only its total time, muted, saying its breakdown wasn't
        recorded.
